16
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:49,110
La la la.

17
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:52,120
He'll try to keep his
sanity with the help

18
00:00:52,110 --> 00:00:55,610
of his robot friends.

19
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:57,120
Robot roll call.
Cambot.

20
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:59,120
You're on.
Gypsy.

21
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:00,630
Oh my stars.

22
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:01,620
Tom Servo.
Check me out.

23
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:02,120
Crow!

24
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:03,630
I'm different!

25
00:01:03,630 --> 00:01:07,630
If you're wondering how he eats
and breathes, and other science

26
00:01:07,630 --> 00:01:12,140
facts, just repeat to
yourself, "It's just a show.

27
00:01:12,130 --> 00:01:22,140
I should really just relax for
Mystery Science Theater 3000."

28
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:34,980
I should really just relax for
Mystery Science Theater 3000."

29
00:01:34,990 --> 00:01:35,990
-Hey, folks.

30
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:38,000
Painting day up here on
the Satellite of Love.

31
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:40,490
Time to spruce up the
galley, the companion ways.

32
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:42,000
-And the can.

33
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:43,500
-And the can, too, yes.

34
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:44,500
I thought I'd, uh--

35
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:45,500
-The can really needs it, man.

36
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:46,500
Let me tell you.

37
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,010
-Wow.

38
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:49,500
OK, I thought I'd try a little
experiment based on the theory

39
00:01:49,510 --> 00:01:52,010
that the color of
the room can affect

40
00:01:52,010 --> 00:01:54,010
the mood of said
room's occupants.

41
00:01:54,010 --> 00:01:56,010
For instance, this
color of green

42
00:01:56,010 --> 00:01:59,520
is used in prisons, because it's
said to calm the inmates down.

43
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:01,020
How does this make
you guys feel?

44
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:03,520
-Well, I feel calm, placid.

45
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,520
Sort of a Jimmy
Carter-like serenity.

46
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:10,030
-I find I have the inexplicable
desire to date Lisa Stansfield.

47
00:02:10,030 --> 00:02:11,530
-That figures.
How about this?

48
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:14,530
This orange is bright, cheery,
said to increase appetite.

49
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:15,530
-Huh.

50
00:02:15,530 --> 00:02:17,040
This may sound odd,
but I feel bitter

51
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:19,030
and ignored, you callous snobs.

52
00:02:19,030 --> 00:02:21,530
-It increases my desire
to date Lisa Stansfield,

53
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:22,540
but in particular,
I want to take

54
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:25,040
her to see competitive curling.

55
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:27,050
-I don't know why I even bother.

56
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:28,040
But let's try one more.

57
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:29,040
How about this one?

58
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:31,040
This is a morose
blackish crimson,

59
00:02:31,050 --> 00:02:34,050
the color of dry blood.

60
00:02:34,050 --> 00:02:36,550
The exact color of
the last room in Poe's

61
00:02:36,550 --> 00:02:38,060
Masque of the Red Death.

62
00:02:38,550 --> 00:02:41,050
One look at it is said to
drive its occupants mad.

63
00:02:41,060 --> 00:02:46,560
Mad, I tell you, mad! [laughing]
How does it make you feel, hm?

64
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:48,570
-Hm.
-Hm?

65
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:49,560
-I'm sticking with
the Stansfield bit.

66
00:02:50,070 --> 00:02:52,570
-Yeah, I think it'd be
perfect for the can.

67
00:02:52,570 --> 00:02:54,570
-No, in there, I'm going
to use this eggshell color.

68
00:02:54,570 --> 00:02:55,570
-Oh!
Help!

69
00:02:55,570 --> 00:02:56,070
-Ah!

70
00:02:56,070 --> 00:02:56,570
Oh, it's horrible.

71
00:02:56,570 --> 00:02:58,580
Get it away, Mike.
-Good.

72
00:02:58,570 --> 00:03:00,070
That'll keep them
out of my biffy.

73
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:01,080
We'll be right back.

74
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:03,080
-Oh, horrible!
-Devils!

75
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:13,730
-And how does this
color make you feel, hm?

76
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:14,720
-Hm.

77
00:03:15,220 --> 00:03:16,720
-It makes me want
to invest everything

78
00:03:16,730 --> 00:03:18,730
I have in steam powered
weaving machines.

79
00:03:18,730 --> 00:03:20,230
-Me too.

80
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:21,730
Man, that's eerie how
colors can influence you.

81
00:03:21,730 --> 00:03:22,230
Wow.

82
00:03:22,730 --> 00:03:24,240
-Oh, uh, Pearl's pestering us.

83
00:03:24,230 --> 00:03:25,730
Yes?

84
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:27,230
-Bloodless Bob to Monkey One.

85
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:29,240
Bloodless Bob to Monkey One.

86
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:31,240
The lemmings are in position.

87
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:33,240
Drop your pay lode.

88
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:34,250
-Oh, hello, Mike.

89
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:37,740
I'm going to start today's
experiment with a little poll.

90
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:43,250
How do you feel about
tactical misdirections?

91
00:03:43,250 --> 00:03:44,760
-Oh, where'd her hand go?

92
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:46,750
-Uh, tactical mis-- uh, hm.

93
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:49,260
Well, I guess, you know, if
it's done for the right reasons,

94
00:03:49,260 --> 00:03:50,260
I can certainly see
using tactical--

95
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:51,260
[engine]
-Hey!

96
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:52,260
-Whoa!

97
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:54,270
-Hey, you used
tactical misdirection

98
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:56,760
so we wouldn't notice you're
secretly spraying poison on us!

99
00:03:56,770 --> 00:03:58,770
-Boy.
-Yeah!

100
00:03:58,770 --> 00:04:01,270
-I don't know what
you're implying, Nelson,

101
00:04:01,270 --> 00:04:03,780
but I don't like
where it's headed.

102
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:07,270
-What I'm implying is that
you spray poison on us-- hey!

103
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:09,280
You did it again!

104
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:11,280
Admit it!

105
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:12,780
-I have a statement.

106
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:14,290
Ahem.

107
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:17,280
In response to various
reports to the contrary,

108
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:19,290
at no time did I
or my associates,

109
00:04:19,290 --> 00:04:22,790
me personally, or myself, spray
any kind of contaminants, nerve

110
00:04:23,290 --> 00:04:24,800
function inhibitors,
or thin bouillons

111
00:04:24,790 --> 00:04:27,790
on the inhabitants of
the Satellite of Love.

112
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:29,300
-OK, Monkey One, this
is Bloodless Bob.

113
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:31,800
Hit them hard with
everything you've got!

114
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:32,800
-Oh, hey.

115
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:34,310
So I did.

116
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:36,300
But what you have
to understand is

117
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:37,810
things were different back then.

118
00:04:37,810 --> 00:04:40,810
Once you frame it in the proper
context, I think you'll--

119
00:04:41,310 --> 00:04:42,810
-That was 30 seconds ago.

120
00:04:42,810 --> 00:04:44,820
-Yeah, and now I've got
several more sets of arms!

121
00:04:44,810 --> 00:04:47,310
I don't even know
which ones are mine!

122
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:48,820
Well, they're all mine, I guess.

123
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:51,320
-And hey, take a
look at these hands!

124
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:53,830
Might as well call
me Bob Huge Hands.

125
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:56,320
-And I've got a Servo
growing out of my back.

126
00:04:56,330 --> 00:04:58,330
Now I'll have to
sleep on my stomach.

127
00:04:58,330 --> 00:04:59,330
-Hey!

128
00:04:59,330 --> 00:05:01,830
-Now you did it again!

129
00:05:01,830 --> 00:05:02,830
-Boy.

130
00:05:02,830 --> 00:05:04,340
-Fine, fine.

131
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:06,830
I'll drop a load of
very expensive, highly

132
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:08,340
experimental antidotes.

133
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:10,340
But you owe me, Nelson.

134
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:12,840
I'm also sending you
a really stupid bat

135
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:15,850
movie called "It
Lives By Night."

136
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:17,340
-Monkey One, give the
babies their bottle.

137
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:22,350
Over.

138
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:25,360
[engine]

139
00:05:25,350 --> 00:05:27,350
-Well, I should dry up and
fall right off you, Mike.

140
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:28,860
-Yeah, sure.
No problem.

141
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:30,360
-Hey, put her there, pal.

142
00:05:30,360 --> 00:05:31,360
-Yeah.

143
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:32,360
-[laughing]

144
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:35,370
[movie sign]

145
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:51,020
-Confound that Pearl.

146
00:05:51,010 --> 00:05:53,010
-Hello?

147
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:56,520
-Well, someone three houses down
runs a vacuum cleaner at night.

148
00:06:00,020 --> 00:06:01,030
-Wow, we're going
to need spotters

149
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:05,020
in order to watch this movie.

150
00:06:05,030 --> 00:06:08,530
-The left eye of Laura Mars.

151
00:06:08,530 --> 00:06:13,040
-[laughing]

152
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,540
-Stellaluna.

153
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:19,040
-I hate these new
aluminum bats, by the way.

154
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:24,040
-Let me go!

155
00:06:24,050 --> 00:06:27,050
-Help!

156
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:27,550
This guy's nuts!

157
00:06:31,050 --> 00:06:33,050
-God, what are we seeing?

158
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:33,560
Ugh.

159
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:38,060
-That's it.

160
00:06:38,060 --> 00:06:41,070
Let's go, pal.

161
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:42,060
-A human!

162
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:44,060
He'll entangle my
feet with his hair!

163
00:06:49,570 --> 00:06:51,080
-Ick, there are sheets involved.

164
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:56,080
-[groaning]

165
00:06:56,080 --> 00:06:58,080
-I hate it when you get
bats in your pajamas.

166
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:02,090
-Yeah.

167
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:05,080
-Oh, Vikings, why?
-Alanis Morissette.

168
00:07:05,590 --> 00:07:07,090
No wonder he's
having a nightmare.

169
00:07:11,090 --> 00:07:12,090
-Johnny.

170
00:07:12,090 --> 00:07:15,590
-Honey, you've got
to cut your toenails.

171
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:18,600
-Johnny.
-You can go home now.

172
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:22,110
-You're yelling in
your sleep again.

173
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:23,600
The same dream?

174
00:07:23,610 --> 00:07:28,610
-Yeah, only Raymond Burr
was in it this time.

175
00:07:28,610 --> 00:07:30,620
-Try to go back to sleep.

176
00:07:30,610 --> 00:07:31,610
-I'll change the bedding.

177
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:41,130
-Now I'm dreaming of credits!

178
00:07:43,630 --> 00:07:46,130
[music playing]

179
00:07:46,130 --> 00:07:48,130
-It's Julee Cruise in concert.

180
00:07:55,640 --> 00:07:57,640
-Boy, Batman has really
let his place go.

181
00:08:02,140 --> 00:08:04,140
-Well, it shouldn't
drink so much coffee.

182
00:08:06,650 --> 00:08:10,660
-Now the credits are
gently hang gliding away.

183
00:08:10,650 --> 00:08:11,650
-It lives by night.

184
00:08:11,650 --> 00:08:14,650
It pulls a long shift
down at the Ford plant.

185
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:19,660
-In fact, the Lou Shaw film.

186
00:08:23,670 --> 00:08:27,170
-Stewart enjoys living on the
north side of many large trees.

187
00:08:31,670 --> 00:08:33,170
-Now, your car is on fire.

188
00:08:33,180 --> 00:08:35,180
I told you we shouldn't
do kebabs on the manifold.

189
00:08:38,180 --> 00:08:39,680
-Music for the heavily sedated.

190
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:44,680
-Well now Bonnie's
not fooling anyone.

191
00:08:44,690 --> 00:08:46,190
It's Bonnie Van Lesbian.

192
00:08:50,690 --> 00:08:53,690
-When picking up hitchhikers
goes horribly wrong.

193
00:08:57,700 --> 00:09:01,710
-Oh, I wish this car
had an eject button.

194
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:03,700
-An early David Berley.

195
00:09:06,210 --> 00:09:09,210
-I've got a job delivering
spooky folk singers.

196
00:09:12,710 --> 00:09:15,210
-Stop it.

197
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:17,220
-(SINGING) Well, I'll be damned.

198
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:18,720
Here comes your ghost again.

199
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:22,720
-Someday I'll tell
you why I'm so smug.

200
00:09:26,730 --> 00:09:29,230
-(SINGING) Take me
out to the ball game.

201
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:37,240
-I'm just gonna drive
it off a cliff, honey.

202
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:44,250
-A guy named Associate Producer
was the Dante Cuccinello?

203
00:09:44,250 --> 00:09:45,250
-Mike.
-What?

204
00:09:45,250 --> 00:09:46,750
He was--
-Stop it.

205
00:09:50,250 --> 00:09:52,760
-A woman and her
guitar were found dead

206
00:09:52,750 --> 00:09:54,750
in a shallow grave
in the desert today.

207
00:09:59,260 --> 00:10:00,770
-Vacation in the Forbidden Zone.

208
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:04,260
-Johnny?

209
00:10:04,270 --> 00:10:05,770
Look at this.

210
00:10:05,770 --> 00:10:08,270
-It's a free range orange.

211
00:10:08,270 --> 00:10:09,770
-What have you got?

212
00:10:09,770 --> 00:10:10,780
-I don't know.

213
00:10:10,770 --> 00:10:12,770
-Oh, it's the
onion of knowledge.

214
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:16,780
-It's cactus [inaudible].

215
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:18,780
-Oh.
Huh.

216
00:10:18,780 --> 00:10:21,790
-Mary Tyler Less.

217
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:25,280
-Man, she's got a lot
of fingers, hasn't she?

218
00:10:25,790 --> 00:10:28,290
-What is it?

219
00:10:28,290 --> 00:10:29,790
-Don't you hear that?

220
00:10:29,790 --> 00:10:31,800
-What?

221
00:10:31,790 --> 00:10:34,290
-I think I heard U2.

222
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:35,300
-Johnny?

223
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:37,800
-Shh, shh.

224
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:40,300
-Duh?

225
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:41,810
-Well, what is it?

226
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:42,800
-Duh.

227
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:45,300
-I don't know.

228
00:10:45,310 --> 00:10:47,310
I hear something, but I--

229
00:10:47,310 --> 00:10:49,310
-I'm too dumb to interpret it.

230
00:10:49,310 --> 00:10:52,820
-That's strange.
-Johnny.

231
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:57,320
-Darn, lost my crutches.

232
00:11:01,820 --> 00:11:02,820
-Come on, bring your thinness.

233
00:11:05,830 --> 00:11:06,330
-Come with me.

234
00:11:06,330 --> 00:11:07,830
We'll surprise him.

235
00:11:11,330 --> 00:11:13,830
-Mutual of Omaha's Mild Kingdom.

236
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:19,340
-It's horrible.

237
00:11:19,340 --> 00:11:20,340
-Couldn't even do one pushup.

238
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:22,340
-Johnny, come on.
Let's get out of here.

239
00:11:22,340 --> 00:11:24,340
-Don't you dare eat
my potato salad.

240
00:11:24,350 --> 00:11:26,850
-Johnny?
Johnny?

241
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:28,350
-I'm sorry, Cath.

242
00:11:28,350 --> 00:11:29,850
I keep forgetting they
turn everybody else off.

243
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:30,860
-They turn me on.

244
00:11:30,850 --> 00:11:33,350
-God, it's ugly.

245
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:34,360
-I'll get rid of it.

246
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:36,360
We can get going.

247
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:41,370
-I'll get it!

248
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:43,860
-Johnny, can't we skip
the caves for today

249
00:11:43,870 --> 00:11:45,370
and go right on to the slopes?

250
00:11:45,370 --> 00:11:48,370
-I'd kind of like to make
the last tour if we can.

251
00:11:48,370 --> 00:11:50,370
-But I've got a lot
of not eating to do.

252
00:11:50,370 --> 00:11:51,880
-Is it so important?

253
00:11:51,870 --> 00:11:53,370
-Well, of course not.

254
00:11:53,380 --> 00:11:55,380
It's just my work.
-[sarcastic mumbling]

255
00:11:55,380 --> 00:11:57,880
-That's what the weekend's
all about, remember?

256
00:11:58,380 --> 00:11:59,380
-I remember something else.

257
00:11:59,380 --> 00:12:02,390
This weekend is also supposed
to be an overdue honeymoon.

258
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:04,880
-We were supposed to
do the humpty hump.

259
00:12:04,890 --> 00:12:05,890
-You're right.

260
00:12:05,890 --> 00:12:06,390
